hi all I hope everybody is doing well at  home happy blessed Ramadan to those who  are fasting this year is going to be  different because for most of us we will  be spending all our time at home but  this can actually be a really positive  thing because that means more time with  your family more healthy home-cooked  meals and also more time to do your  workouts so in encouraging all of you to  continue to stay active at home I've put  together a 30 day workout playlist for  you on my youtube channel the link is in  the description box below a lot of you  have also been asking questions such as  how frequent should I workout how long  should my workout session be what type  of exercises to do and when is the best  time to workout so in this video I'm  going to share with you seven tips on  how you can continue to stay active  during Ramadan let's be realistic during  the month of Ramadan your goal is to try  and maintain your current fitness level  and lean muscle tissue so this is not  the time to be trying out new crazy  workouts for those of you who are  already exercising regularly you want to  continue your fitness routine at a lower  intensity and shorter duration to  prevent muscle loss and weight gain you  should not be training at 100% intensity  and then stuffing yourself at night how  many times per week should I work out I  would say aim for at least the minimum  to maintain lean muscle tissue which is  three times per week three times every  alternate day would be ideal but if you  have been exercising very regularly then  of course you can stick to your five to  six times a week routine what you should  be doing is plan your workout this prior  to Ramadan for instance it can be Monday  Wednesday and Friday by planning ahead  and committing to the schedule this  would give you the discipline and ensure  that you complete every workout session  without excuses  next question how long should your  workout session be I would say stick to  at least 20 to 45 minutes per session if  you were to do a slightly more intense  workout such as a hip session then keep  it between 20 to 30 minutes long if it's  a light to moderate exercise such as  holidays or even strength training then  you can even go up to 45 minutes  how intense should your workout be  simply put your Ramadan workout should  be about 30 to 50 percent less intense  than how you would normally work out  outside of Ramadan remember this is not  the time to increase the intensity of  your workouts or to try new crazy  exercises stick to the workout routine  you have been doing and regress by  decreasing the weights number of  repetitions increase the rest time and  you can even choose easier options such  as a normal squat instead of jump squats  the next question is probably what most  of you would want to know when is the  best time to workout well it is  definitely not when you are fasting or  starving only train when you know you  can have food and fluid in your body  training when fasting can be more  harmful than beneficial training when  your body is in starvation and  dehydration can decrease strength and  mathematics significantly a research has  shown that by dehydrating a muscle by as  little as 3 percent can cause a 12  percent loss in strength and that's not  what we want so the best times to  workout are after iftar this is an  excellent time to workout because you  have eaten a small meal and taken in  liquids which can act as a pre-workout  meal however it really depends on how  much time you have between iftar and  prayers if you choose to work out after  iftar then aim for a shorter 20 to 30  minutes session with a slightly higher  intensity after taraweeh prayers if it's  not too late this is also a really great  time because right after your workout  you're able to consume a bigger  post-workout meal this meal is  for muscle recovery muscle growth and  also can help with fat loss and the foot  one is before sajo if you have the  mental discipline to get up really early  in the morning and spit in a workout  session your morning workout can give  you more energy throughout the day drink  plenty of water and you can even have a  small snack 30 minutes prior to  exercising and after your workout be  sure to keep yourself hydrated drink  lots of water and eat a light  nutrient-dense soccer meal to keep you  going through the day another most  frequently asked question what type of  exercises should i do since ramadan is  when you lose muscle tone it is more  important to focus on strength training  / cardio aim to do strength training  three times a week and if you really  want to fit in a cardio session then  keep it to two days a week in for 20 to  30 minutes session this is to preserve  as much muscle mass as possible and the  final tip is just for the ladies ladies  the best time to workout during Ramadan  and to increase your workout intensity  is that one week break you get if you're  wondering whether should I work out when  I'm on my period well the answer is yes  if you feel absolutely fine then perform  your workout as usual and give it your  100% effort in however you do experience  some a cramp or discomfort then take it  easy and do gentle exercises so remember  to enjoy Ramadan this year use these  guidelines and see what works best for  you and of course make sure your  exercise does not take away the main  focus of Ramadan in the end the focus of  Ramadan is spiritual development I also  have a Ramadan meal plan for those of  you who might be interested to follow  all the links are in my description box  below so have a blessed Ramadan stay  home and stay safe remember to give this  video a thumbs up share this video and  subscribe to my youtube channel for a  lot more home workout videos and also  healthy recipes and I will see you next  time  all the best
 

